1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,923
Chai Corner has six people
now, and Ravi pays them well.

2
00:00:04,223 --> 00:00:06,852
But the team is quiet,
and the chai is slow.

3
00:00:07,402 --> 00:00:09,151
Ravi offers more money.

4
00:00:09,151 --> 00:00:10,398
Nothing changes.

5
00:00:10,698 --> 00:00:13,803
Then one evening, he simply
sits down and listens.

6
00:00:14,103 --> 00:00:16,421
Next morning, everyone works faster.

7
00:00:17,071 --> 00:00:18,739
Money was not missing.

8
00:00:18,739 --> 00:00:20,134
His attention was.

9
00:00:20,834 --> 00:00:23,390
This is the human relations approach.

10
00:00:23,690 --> 00:00:27,623
People work better when they feel
noticed, valued, and part of a team.

11
00:00:28,223 --> 00:00:32,444
It comes from Elton Mayo, a Harvard
professor, and his colleague Fritz

12
00:00:32,444 --> 00:00:33,445
Roethlisberger.

13
00:00:34,045 --> 00:00:38,298
They explained the Hawthorne studies,
at Western Electric's factory near

14
00:00:38,298 --> 00:00:39,198
Chicago.

15
00:00:39,301 --> 00:00:44,250
From 1924 to 1932, in four stages.

16
00:00:44,850 --> 00:00:45,811
Stage one.

17
00:00:45,811 --> 00:00:47,336
The lighting tests.

18
00:00:47,636 --> 00:00:48,787
Brighter lights?

19
00:00:48,787 --> 00:00:49,948
Output went up.

20
00:00:50,248 --> 00:00:51,263
Dimmer lights?

21
00:00:51,263 --> 00:00:52,724
Output still went up.

22
00:00:53,024 --> 00:00:54,668
So it was not the light.

23
00:00:54,668 --> 00:00:56,012
Someone was watching.

24
00:00:56,512 --> 00:00:57,448
Stage two.

25
00:00:57,448 --> 00:00:59,600
The relay assembly test room.

26
00:00:59,900 --> 00:01:02,998
A small group of women
assembled telephone parts.

27
00:01:03,298 --> 00:01:07,797
Researchers changed their breaks,
snacks, and hours, and asked their views.

28
00:01:08,097 --> 00:01:11,924
Output kept rising, even when
the old conditions came back.

29
00:01:12,424 --> 00:01:13,391
Stage three.

30
00:01:13,391 --> 00:01:15,016
The interview programme.

31
00:01:15,316 --> 00:01:19,293
Over twenty thousand workers were
asked how they felt about work.

32
00:01:19,593 --> 00:01:22,999
Being listened to made many
feel better about their jobs.

33
00:01:23,499 --> 00:01:24,494
Stage four.

34
00:01:24,494 --> 00:01:26,158
The bank wiring room.

35
00:01:26,458 --> 00:01:30,174
Fourteen men wired equipment
for telephone exchanges.

36
00:01:30,474 --> 00:01:34,294
They formed an informal group,
with their own unwritten rules.

37
00:01:34,594 --> 00:01:36,759
Don't work too fast, or too slow.

38
00:01:36,759 --> 00:01:39,444
The group set the pace, not the pay plan.

39
00:01:39,944 --> 00:01:41,545
So, what did Mayo learn?

40
00:01:41,895 --> 00:01:42,795
One.

41
00:01:42,424 --> 00:01:45,517
Workers are social beings
who want to belong.

42
00:01:45,817 --> 00:01:46,717
Two.

43
00:01:46,340 --> 00:01:48,012
Attention raises output.

44
00:01:48,012 --> 00:01:50,483
This is called the Hawthorne effect.

45
00:01:50,783 --> 00:01:51,683
Three.

46
00:01:51,417 --> 00:01:54,346
Informal groups shape
how hard people work.

47
00:01:54,646 --> 00:01:55,546
And four.

48
00:01:55,455 --> 00:01:58,700
Money matters, but it is
not the only motivator.

49
00:01:59,200 --> 00:02:03,878
Back at Chai Corner, Ravi now holds a
five minute team chat every morning.

50
00:02:04,178 --> 00:02:07,077
He thanks Asha by name
for her ginger chai.

51
00:02:07,377 --> 00:02:10,928
And the team now serves sixty
cups an hour, up from forty.

52
00:02:11,578 --> 00:02:12,478
Quick check.

53
00:02:12,471 --> 00:02:15,259
Ravi notices his team,
and they work harder.

54
00:02:15,259 --> 00:02:16,630
What is this called?

55
00:02:18,530 --> 00:02:19,855
The Hawthorne effect!

56
00:02:19,855 --> 00:02:22,336
People work better when they feel noticed.

57
00:02:22,936 --> 00:02:23,945
So, remember.

58
00:02:24,245 --> 00:02:30,533
The Hawthorne studies ran from
1924 to 1932, near Chicago.

59
00:02:30,883 --> 00:02:34,673
Mayo showed that workers are
social beings, not machines.

60
00:02:35,023 --> 00:02:39,058
Attention, belonging, and good
teams raise output, not just money.

61
00:02:39,658 --> 00:02:44,412
Next time, we'll see a business as one
big system, in the systems approach.
